1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Before your team adopts an AI coding agent, the most useful question

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,000
isn't "does it work?" It's: who checks the result, and how much

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
does that checking cost? Several recent sources raise that question from different

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
angles. Here's what they actually say—and what they don't.

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,088
The human role. GitHub's skills guidance argues that working with AI agents

6
00:00:19,088 --> 00:00:23,176
is a high-skill coordination job: defining problems clearly, evaluating generated code, and

7
00:00:23,176 --> 00:00:27,264
deciding what's ready to ship. That framing comes from a company selling

8
00:00:27,264 --> 00:00:31,352
agent tools, so treat it as a source claim, not an independent

9
00:00:31,352 --> 00:00:35,440
finding. But the practical point holds: delegating implementation does not remove responsibility

10
00:00:35,440 --> 00:00:39,527
for the result. Prompting as a skill. In a Latent Space podcast

11
00:00:39,527 --> 00:00:43,615
interview, Anthropic's Thariq Shihipar described prompting as more like writing for a

12
00:00:43,615 --> 00:00:47,703
specific audience than entering a search query—something with a high skill ceiling

13
00:00:47,703 --> 00:00:51,791
that depends on building a mental model of how the agent thinks,

14
00:00:51,791 --> 00:00:55,879
knowing your domain vocabulary, and actively hunting for what you haven't written

15
00:00:55,879 --> 00:00:59,967
down yet. He calls those unknown unknowns the most important gap. His

16
00:00:59,967 --> 00:01:04,055
example: ask an agent to design a game without any game-design background,

17
00:01:04,055 --> 00:01:08,143
and you may not be able to tell whether the output is

18
00:01:08,143 --> 00:01:12,231
any good. This is a vendor perspective, not a measured study—but the

19
00:01:12,231 --> 00:01:16,319
warning applies any time you generate code in a domain you don't

20
00:01:16,319 --> 00:01:17,000
understand well.

21
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,757
How much process do you actually need? A Stack Overflow production field

22
00:01:21,757 --> 00:01:26,514
guide published in early October proposes a staged maturity model: determinism, evaluation,

23
00:01:26,514 --> 00:01:31,270
confidence handling, safety, operability, and a full production build. Its self-assessment asks

24
00:01:31,270 --> 00:01:36,027
things like: can the agent only propose changes, with a separate step

25
00:01:36,027 --> 00:01:40,784
to apply them? Does a prompt change that breaks quality fail your

26
00:01:40,784 --> 00:01:45,541
CI pipeline? Can you stop automated decisions in seconds without a deploy?

27
00:01:45,541 --> 00:01:50,297
This is practitioner guidance, not an empirically validated minimum for student projects.

28
00:01:50,297 --> 00:01:55,054
The guide's own sequencing principle translates well even if the full checklist

29
00:01:55,054 --> 00:01:59,811
doesn't: a prototype generating boilerplate needs lighter controls than a service handling

30
00:01:59,811 --> 00:02:01,000
authentication or payments.

31
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:05,560
One concrete cost. GitHub's push-protection announcement illustrates what scaled agent output means

32
00:02:05,560 --> 00:02:10,120
for a specific risk. According to GitHub, push protection today stops roughly

33
00:02:10,120 --> 00:02:14,680
30 percent of newly detected secrets before they enter repository history; the

34
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:19,240
remaining 70 percent are found after the credential is already exposed, requiring

35
00:02:19,240 --> 00:02:23,800
human cleanup that doesn't scale with code volume. A new AI classifier

36
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:28,360
called ModernBERT could more than double prevention coverage—but it will consume AI

37
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:32,920
credits and was in private preview at announcement time. The existing protection

38
00:02:32,920 --> 00:02:37,480
feature and the proposed new classifier are not the same thing. Plan

39
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:39,000
around what's available today.

40
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:43,522
Infrastructure promises are still promises. Cloudflare and Deno announced that the Deno

41
00:02:43,522 --> 00:02:48,043
team is joining Cloudflare, with a plan to merge their two server

42
00:02:48,043 --> 00:02:52,565
runtimes and make self-hosting a first-class supported experience. More announcements are promised

43
00:02:52,565 --> 00:02:57,087
in coming months. You can self-host either runtime today—but that existing capability

44
00:02:57,087 --> 00:03:01,609
is distinct from the merged experience being described. Monitor this work rather

45
00:03:01,609 --> 00:03:05,000
than planning a migration around something that hasn't shipped.

46
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:10,182
A gap worth naming. No source here measures adoption costs or outcomes

47
00:03:10,182 --> 00:03:15,364
specifically for student teams. The vendor sources have commercial interests, and the

48
00:03:15,364 --> 00:03:20,545
field guide offers advice rather than measured student outcomes. Novice maintainers, accessibility

49
00:03:20,545 --> 00:03:24,000
needs, and teams without enterprise budgets remain underrepresented.

50
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:28,140
So here is the practical sequence: pick one bounded task whose result

51
00:03:28,140 --> 00:03:32,280
you can verify yourself, identify who on your team owns that check,

52
00:03:32,280 --> 00:03:36,420
and match controls to the actual risk level. The question to keep

53
00:03:36,420 --> 00:03:40,560
asking: when you find a defect the agent introduced silently, how long

54
00:03:40,560 --> 00:03:44,700
did that take to catch—and was the time saved earlier worth it?
